Subject: [SE3-P7] mf: smooth the audio renderer's presentation time

Wine's audio drivers (winepulse) advance the IAudioClock position one device
period (10 ms) at a time, where Windows reports a device-interpolated
position. The streaming audio renderer maps that position straight to
presentation time, so an audio-clocked presentation clock moves in 10 ms
steps: 60 fps video is shown on an uneven 20/20/10 ms cadence and a 60 Hz
consumer can miss every third frame, depending on phase.

Report the performance counter plus the largest (time - counter) offset
seen instead. Readings taken just after a step approach the true offset, so
the result is smooth, never runs backwards and is never more than one device
period ahead of the reported position. The estimate decays by 1 ms per
second to follow clock drift. It is reset whenever the timeline restarts
(seek, stop, flush) and not applied while paused, scrubbing, starting or
reporting a seek target. With an interpolating driver, as on Windows, the
reported time is unchanged.

---
diff --git a/dlls/mf/sar.c b/dlls/mf/sar.c
--- a/dlls/mf/sar.c
+++ b/dlls/mf/sar.c
@@ -114,8 +114,22 @@
     UINT64 position;
     UINT64 audio_clock_frequency;
     float rate;
+    /* Correlated time smoothing, see audio_renderer_smooth_time(). */
+    REFERENCE_TIME device_period;
+    LONGLONG smooth_offset;
+    LONGLONG smooth_counter;
+    LONGLONG smooth_time;
+    BOOL smooth_offset_valid;
+    BOOL smooth_time_valid;
 };
 
+/* Call wherever the presentation timeline restarts: seek, stop, flush, reset. */
+static void audio_renderer_reset_smooth_time(struct audio_renderer *renderer)
+{
+    renderer->smooth_offset_valid = FALSE;
+    renderer->smooth_time_valid = FALSE;
+}
+
